            <article class="article-body fs-6 lh-lg">
                <p class="lead">There are two kinds of award ceremonies in this industry. The first is the gala ballroom — black tie, a stage in Limassol, trophies for the giants. The second is the expo floor: fluorescent lights, a crowd of IBs and affiliates, and an award handed out between keynote sessions. This week, Forex Expo Dubai's awards went decisively to the second kind's home turf. Two challenger brokers walked away with the floor's biggest prizes, and that is worth paying attention to — just not for the reason the press releases suggest.</p>

                <h2 class="mt-5 mb-3">ePlanet Brokers: 'Best Broker 2026', won on the floor</h2>

                <p><strong>ePlanet Brokers</strong> was named <strong>'Best Broker 2026'</strong> at Forex Expo Dubai 2026, with the award presented on 22 September, the first day of the expo at the Dubai World Trade Centre. If the name doesn't ring a bell, that is precisely the point — ePlanet is a growing brokerage, not one of the decade-old giants, and it used the expo floor as its coming-out stage.</p>

                <p>The award lands at the end of a year of visible groundwork. The company expanded its network of introducing brokers and business partners, built out its trading and operational infrastructure, and put money into customer support and account management. It also published an <strong>H1 2026 Transparency Report</strong> — sharing operational data on KYC, deposits, withdrawals, trade execution, platform reliability and customer support — and added <strong>Nebula Ventures Ltd in Vanuatu</strong> to its regulatory structure alongside its existing Comoros entity.</p>

                <p>Read that list carefully: transparency reporting, infrastructure, support, another licence. None of it is glamorous. All of it is exactly the kind of unglamorous work a challenger has to show to convince an IB to send clients its way. The expo was where that pitch got its public moment — the company spent the two-day event working Booth 06, holding meetings and building connections in what it describes as its growing <strong>MENA presence</strong>, including an Arabic-speaking client and partner network. 'Best Broker 2026' was the receipt.</p>

                <h2 class="mt-5 mb-3">STARTRADER: 'Best Fintech Forex Broker'</h2>

                <p><strong>STARTRADER</strong> took <strong>'Best Fintech Forex Broker'</strong> at the Forex Expo Dubai Awards, the ceremony organised by HQMENA and presented during the expo on 22–23 September. The company came to Dubai as a <strong>Global Sponsor</strong> — the top sponsorship tier — and worked Booth 161 hard: a suspended LED canopy, a curved video wall carrying its wordmark alongside its <strong>Official NBA Partner</strong> and <strong>Porsche Carrera Cup Middle East</strong> branding, a wall of NBA team emblems, a basketball-column installation and a branded mascot display.</p>

                <p>But the award citation was about product, not booth theatre. The recognition followed a year in which STARTRADER expanded its <strong>24/7 trading offering</strong>, built on the argument that clients shouldn't be constrained by traditional market hours. CEO Peter Karsten's keynote tackled <strong>pre-IPO trading risks</strong> — an unusually forward-looking topic for an expo stage — and he joined a podcast session titled 'The Edge of Reason: Trading Markets 2027' on where market structure is heading.</p>

                <p>"Awards are milestones, not destinations," Karsten said. "What this one tells us is that the direction we chose, building around our clients rather than around convention, is the right one. That is the standard we will keep holding ourselves to." It is the kind of quote every winner gives — but the substance behind it is harder to dismiss: 24/7 trading, sponsorship of two major sports properties, and a keynote about pre-IPO risk rather than another 'we love traders' platitude.</p>

                <h2 class="mt-5 mb-3">An honest word about expo awards</h2>

                <p>Let's be straight about what these awards are. They are <strong>organiser-presented awards</strong> (HQMENA runs the Forex Expo Dubai Awards), handed out at a commercial event where the winners also paid for prominent booths and, in STARTRADER's case, the top sponsorship tier. Nobody should confuse an expo award with a fully independent, blind-judged industry verdict. Press releases will not tell you that; we will.</p>

                <p>But that doesn't make them meaningless. It makes them a <em>different kind of signal</em>. What an expo award measures is <strong>commitment to a market</strong>: the money spent on the booth, the keynote slot taken seriously, the partners courted face to face, the regional story — MENA, Arabic-speaking, Dubai-based relationships — told where the region's IBs and affiliates can check it in person. In an industry where brokers disappear offshore overnight, showing up on the floor and winning in front of the room is a checkable act. A press release can claim anything; a two-day expo presence costs real money and puts real people in front of real partners.</p>

                <h2 class="mt-5 mb-3">The Dubai audition</h2>

                <p>There is a reason both winners' stories run through Dubai. The Gulf's retail trading scene has become the industry's growth corridor, and the expo is its audition room. ePlanet's MENA and Arabic-speaking push, STARTRADER's sponsorship machinery, MultiBank's institutional double at the same expo — everyone is telling the same story: <strong>Dubai is where you prove you belong.</strong></p>

                <p>For traders, the right way to read these results is as a <strong>watch-list, not a verdict</strong>. A challenger winning 'Best Broker 2026' on the expo floor doesn't settle the debate — it opens one: is ePlanet's transparency report backed by client experience? Is STARTRADER's 24/7 offering genuinely seamless? Those are questions only trading and time can answer. But the expo awards tell you <em>where to look</em>, and that is genuinely useful in an industry with hundreds of brokers competing for attention.</p>

                <h2 class="mt-5 mb-3">Why this matters for the ProFX Awards</h2>

                <p>The lesson of the expo floor is that recognition works best when it is tied to checkable effort — presence, transparency, product. That's the standard any awards programme worth its salt has to hold itself to. When we open nominations, we look for the brokers, fintechs and trading providers putting in the work the industry can verify — on the floor, in their reports, in their products — not just the ones with the biggest booths.</p>

                <p>Nominations for <strong>ProFX Awards {{ Helper::awardYear() }}</strong> are open, and the ceremony takes place on {{ Helper::awardEventDate() }} in Dubai. If you know a broker or fintech company whose effort deserves industry-wide recognition, <a href="{{ url('/nomination') }}">submit a nomination</a> — the categories this year reward exactly the kind of substance the expo floor put on display.</p>
